### 4. TECHNICAL APPROACH

First inspect the capabilities of your environment.

If you can create and run a conventional web project, use the following default stack unless your environment requires another supported approach:

- Frontend: React with TypeScript.
- Framework: Next.js or an equivalent supported React framework.
- Styling: Tailwind CSS or a consistent modern CSS system.
- Icons: Lucide or another established icon library.
- Charts: Recharts or a comparable charting library.
- Form validation: Zod or equivalent.
- Persistence for the standalone prototype: localStorage or IndexedDB, with a clean data-access abstraction.
- Testing: suitable unit and end-to-end tests where tooling is available.

You may use a simpler stack if it produces a more reliable, genuinely runnable demonstration in your environment.

Do not introduce unnecessary complexity, paid dependencies, external services, or infrastructure simply to make the architecture look sophisticated.

#### Architecture principles

Separate the application into clear components and modules.

Use reusable UI components, consistent data models, centralised validation, and predictable state management.

Separate business rules from presentation code.

Use TypeScript types or equivalent data contracts for the main entities.

Avoid duplicating business logic in multiple screens.

Design the data model so a future production implementation can replace local prototype persistence with a secure backend and database without having to redesign every screen.

Never put API keys, database service-role keys, passwords, or other secrets into frontend code.

IMPORTANT: This is a demonstration prototype. Do not falsely claim that localStorage is a secure production database, that mock authentication provides real security, or that a local browser deployment supports multiple staff sharing live data.

If a backend is supported and appropriate, it may be used, but document setup requirements and avoid making the demo depend on an unconfigured service.

